      "files": {
        "92118ebf-52cc-41ca-bdef-a977a7f90a5d": {
          "id": "92118ebf-52cc-41ca-bdef-a977a7f90a5d",
          "name": "index.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n    <title>3D Lighting Scene</title>\n    <style>\n        body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; min-height: 100vh; background-color: #333; font-family: sans-serif; color: #eee; }\n        canvas { display: block; border: 2px solid #555; background-color: #000; }\n        h1 { position: absolute; top: 20px; color: #fff; text-align: center; width: 100%; }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n    <h1>3D Lighting Experiment</h1>\n    <canvas id=\"threeJsCanvas\"></canvas>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": true
        },
        "7b726606-9383-48b3-b96f-ba990f920ee6": {
          "id": "7b726606-9383-48b3-b96f-ba990f920ee6",
          "name": "script.js",
          "contents": "// This script simulates a basic 3D scene setup for demonstrating lighting.\n// It provides a simplified API resembling Three.js for educational purposes.\n// The actual 3D rendering is handled by the Web Lab environment.\n\n// --- Scene Setup (Simulated) ---\nconst scene = {\n    children: [], // Where objects and lights are added\n    add: function(object) {\n        this.children.push(object);\n        console.log(`Added object to scene:`, object);\n    }\n};\n\nconst camera = {\n    position: { x: 0, y: 1, z: 5 }, // Slightly above the scene\n    lookAt: { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 }\n};\n\nconst renderer = {\n    domElement: null, // Will be set after document is ready\n    setSize: function(width, height) {\n        if (this.domElement) {\n            this.domElement.width = width;\n            this.domElement.height = height;\n        }\n        console.log(`Renderer size set to ${width}x${height}`);\n    },\n    render: function(scene, camera) {\n        // This is a *highly simplified* 2D representation for visualization.\n        // In a real 3D engine, much more complex rendering would occur based on lights.\n        console.log(\"--- Rendering Scene ---\");\n        const ctx = this.domElement.getContext('2d');\n        const width = this.domElement.width;\n        const height = this.domElement.height;\n\n        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);\n        ctx.fillStyle = '#333'; // Dark background\n        ctx.fillRect(0, 0, width, height);\n\n        const scale = 50; // Pixels per unit\n        const offsetX = width / 2;\n        const offsetY = height / 2;\n\n        // Draw objects\n        scene.children.filter(obj => obj.type === 'Mesh').forEach(obj => {\n            ctx.save();\n            ctx.translate(offsetX + obj.position.x * scale, offsetY - obj.position.y * scale); // Y-axis inverted for canvas vs 3D\n            ctx.fillStyle = obj.material.color;\n            ctx.strokeStyle = '#fff';\n            ctx.lineWidth = 1;\n\n            if (obj.geometry.type === 'BoxGeometry') {\n                ctx.fillRect(-25, -25, 50, 50); // Centered box\n                ctx.strokeRect(-25, -25, 50, 50);\n            } else if (obj.geometry.type === 'SphereGeometry') {\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 30, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n            }\n            ctx.restore();\n        });\n\n        // Draw lights (simplified indicators)\n        scene.children.filter(obj => obj.type && obj.type.includes('Light')).forEach(obj => {\n            ctx.save();\n            ctx.fillStyle = obj.color;\n            ctx.strokeStyle = '#fff';\n            ctx.lineWidth = 1;\n\n            if (obj.type === 'PointLight' && obj.position) {\n                ctx.translate(offsetX + obj.position.x * scale, offsetY - obj.position.y * scale);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('P', -3, 3); // Label for Point Light\n            } else if (obj.type === 'DirectionalLight' && obj.direction) {\n                // Indicate a directional light from a corner, with an arrow\n                ctx.translate(width * 0.15, height * 0.15);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('D', -3, 3); // Label for Directional Light\n                // Draw an arrow indicating direction\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.moveTo(0, 0);\n                // Simplify direction representation for 2D. Let's just point from origin of marker\n                const arrowLength = 20;\n                ctx.lineTo(obj.direction.x * arrowLength, -obj.direction.y * arrowLength); // Y inverted\n                ctx.strokeStyle = obj.color;\n                ctx.stroke();\n            } else if (obj.type === 'AmbientLight') {\n                // Indicate ambient light with a larger circle or fill\n                ctx.translate(width * 0.85, height * 0.15);\n                ctx.beginPath();\n                ctx.arc(0, 0, 8, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n                ctx.fill();\n                ctx.stroke();\n                ctx.fillStyle = '#000'; // Text color\n                ctx.fillText('A', -3, 3); // Label for Ambient Light\n            }\n            ctx.restore();\n        });\n\n        console.log(\"--- End Rendering Scene ---\");\n    }\n};\n\n// --- Helper for Vector-like operations (simulated) ---\nfunction Vector3(x = 0, y = 0, z = 0) {\n    this.x = x;\n    this.y = y;\n    this.z = z;\n}\nVector3.prototype.set = function(x, y, z) {\n    this.x = x;\n    this.y = y;\n    this.z = z;\n    return this;\n};\nVector3.prototype.normalize = function() {\n    const length = Math.sqrt(this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y + this.z * this.z);\n    if (length > 0) {\n        this.x /= length;\n        this.y /= length;\n        this.z /= length;\n    }\n    return this;\n};\n\n\n// --- Geometry and Material (Simulated) ---\nfunction BoxGeometry(width, height, depth) {\n    this.type = 'BoxGeometry';\n    this.width = width; this.height = height; this.depth = depth;\n}\n\nfunction SphereGeometry(radius, widthSegments, heightSegments) {\n    this.type = 'SphereGeometry';\n    this.radius = radius;\n}\n\nfunction MeshStandardMaterial(parameters) {\n    this.type = 'MeshStandardMaterial';\n    // Convert Three.js-like hex color to CSS color string\n    this.color = parameters.color ? `#${(parameters.color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}` : '#ffffff';\n}\n\nfunction Mesh(geometry, material) {\n    this.type = 'Mesh';\n    this.geometry = geometry;\n    this.material = material;\n    this.position = new Vector3();\n    this.rotation = new Vector3(); // For completeness\n}\n\n// --- Light Types (Simulated) ---\n// Students will use these constructors\nfunction AmbientLight(color = 0x404040, intensity = 1) { // Default to a dim grey\n    this.type = 'AmbientLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`; // Convert hex to css color\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n}\n\nfunction PointLight(color = 0xffffff, intensity = 1, distance = 0, decay = 1) {\n    this.type = 'PointLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`;\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n    this.position = new Vector3();\n}\n\nfunction DirectionalLight(color = 0xffffff, intensity = 1) {\n    this.type = 'DirectionalLight';\n    this.color = `#${(color & 0xFFFFFF).toString(16).padStart(6, '0')}`;\n    this.intensity = intensity;\n    // In Three.js, direction is implied by position vs. target.\n    // Here, we'll give it an explicit 'direction' vector for simplicity.\n    this.direction = new Vector3(0, -1, 0); // Default to shining down\n}\n\n// --- Initialize Scene ---\nfunction init() {\n    renderer.domElement = document.getElementById('threeJsCanvas');\n    if (!renderer.domElement) {\n        console.error(\"Canvas element not found!\");\n        return;\n    }\n    renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth * 0.8, window.innerHeight * 0.8); // Adjust size for better fit\n\n    // Create objects\n    const cube = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1), new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xff0000 }));\n    cube.position.set(-1, 0, 0); // Position the cube to the left\n    scene.add(cube);\n\n    const sphere = new Mesh(new SphereGeometry(0.7, 32, 32), new MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x0000ff }));\n    sphere.position.set(1, 0, 0); // Position the sphere to the right\n    scene.add(sphere);\n\n    // --- Student's Light Code Goes Here ---\n    // Task 1: Add an AmbientLight\n    // An AmbientLight uniformly illuminates all objects in the scene from all directions.\n    // It has a 'color' (e.g., 0x404040 for dim gray) and an 'intensity' (e.g., 0.5).\n    // Uncomment the following lines and adjust the values:\n    /*\n    const ambientLight = new AmbientLight(0x404040, 0.5);\n    scene.add(ambientLight);\n    */\n\n\n    // Task 2: Add a PointLight\n    // A PointLight emits light from a single point in all directions, like a light bulb.\n    // It has 'color', 'intensity', and a 'position' (an X, Y, Z coordinate).\n    // Use the .set(x, y, z) method on its 'position' property.\n    // Uncomment the following lines and adjust the values:\n    /*\n    const pointLight = new PointLight(0xffffff, 1); // White light, full intensity\n    pointLight.position.set(0, 2, 2); // Position: X=0, Y=2 (up), Z=2 (forward)\n    scene.add(pointLight);\n    */\n\n\n    // Task 3: Add a DirectionalLight\n    // A DirectionalLight simulates a distant light source like the sun; its rays are parallel.\n    // It has 'color', 'intensity', and a 'direction' vector.\n    // Use the .set(x, y, z).normalize() method on its 'direction' property.\n    // Normalizing ensures the direction vector has a length of 1, which is good practice.\n    // Uncomment the following lines and adjust the values:\n    /*\n    const directionalLight = new DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.8); // White light, 80% intensity\n    directionalLight.direction.set(1, 1, 1).normalize(); // Shining from top-right-front\n    scene.add(directionalLight);\n    */\n\n    // Initial render based on the current scene state\n    renderer.render(scene, camera);\n}\n\n// This function would typically be an animation loop in a real 3D application.\n// For this lesson, we only need to render once after setup.\nfunction animate() {\n    // requestAnimationFrame(animate);\n    // renderer.render(scene, camera);\n}\n\n// Initialize the scene when the script loads\ninit();",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "92118ebf-52cc-41ca-bdef-a977a7f90a5d",
        "7b726606-9383-48b3-b96f-ba990f920ee6"
      ]
    },
    "long_instructions": "You've learned about the different types of lights used in 3D scenes. Now it's your turn to add them to a simple scene! In this level, you'll work with `script.js` to introduce ambient, point, and directional lights.\r\n\r\n1.  **Open `script.js`:** Locate the section marked `// --- Student's Light Code Goes Here ---`.\r\n2.  **Add an Ambient Light:** Uncomment the `AmbientLight` code. Experiment with different `color` values (e.g., `0x404040` for a dim gray or `0xff0000` for red) and `intensity` (0 to 1) to see how it uniformly brightens the scene. Remember to add it to the `scene`.\r\n3.  **Add a Point Light:** Uncomment the `PointLight` code. Adjust its `color`, `intensity`, and `position`. The `position.set(x, y, z)` method places the light source at specific coordinates. Notice how a point light creates a localized glow.\r\n4.  **Add a Directional Light:** Uncomment the `DirectionalLight` code. Change its `color`, `intensity`, and `direction`. The `direction.set(x, y, z).normalize()` method defines the direction the light rays are coming from. Think about how this mimics sunlight.\r\n\r\nObserve the simplified 2D representation of your scene on the canvas, and check the console for logs about the lights you've added. Pay attention to how each light's properties affect the overall illumination and the markers indicating the light's presence or direction.",
    "generate_outline": "Students will modify a given Web Lab 2 3D scene to add and configure ambient, point, and directional light sources. They should observe how each light type changes the appearance of objects."
  },
